        "summary": "The Strait of Hormuz remains under acute operational strain. IMF PortWatch recorded 1 transit on 2026-08-09, against a pre-crisis baseline of 73 per day; that figure is PortWatch's most recently published day and carries a multi-day lag by design. Separately, port arrivals across the Hormuz-area ports on both the Persian Gulf and Gulf of Oman sides reached 401 vessels in the most recent 24 hours, a distinct metric that cannot be compared directly to the PortWatch transit count. At 11:53 UTC, 487 vessels were holding position away from berth, a figure that excludes ships within 25km of a working port. Brent stood at $88.52, up 1.81% over 24 hours, as of 11:53 UTC; futures markets are closed and that delta is a pre-close carry, not a live signal. The site's Crisis Pressure index holds in the extreme band, flat over 24 hours, with physical transit deviation as the top driver; the Escalation Forecast sits in the elevated band, also flat, with Polymarket escalation contracts leading. Those two indices diverge by design: the state composite reflects current operational reality while the forecast composite incorporates forward-looking signals. On the event side, Reuters reports Iran attacked an ADNOC vessel transiting the strait, per gCaptain's Reuters relay; gCaptain also reports a broader pattern of mounting ship attacks. Iranian state media claims, relayed by a single outlet and not independently confirmed, allege Qatar is holding three missing pilots. Iran and Oman have separately agreed on a new shipping route, per azernews.az, though that report is unverified and single-source. Thirteen events were indexed in the past 24 hours.",
